  "html": "<article id=\"s-1\" class=\"card gx-slide\">\n    <div class=\"gx-slide-tag\"><b>Roadmap</b> · how the modules connect</div>\n    <h3>The full map</h3>\n    <figure class=\"gx-diagram\">\n<pre class=\"mermaid\">flowchart LR\n  M0[\"0 · Neural net basics\"] --> M1[\"1 · Architecture zoo\"]\n  M1 --> M2[\"2 · LLM internals\"]\n  M2 --> M3[\"3 · LLM APIs + context\"]\n  M3 --> M4[\"4 · RAG + evals\"]\n  M3 --> M5[\"5 · Agents + MCP\"]\n  M4 --> M5\n  M2 --> M6[\"6 · Fine-tuning + alignment\"]\n  M5 --> M7[\"7 · Serving + LLMOps + security\"]\n  M6 --> M7\n  M1 --> M8[\"8 · Multimodal\"]\n  M7 --> M9[\"9 · Capstone\"]\n  M8 --> M9\n  M4 --> M10[\"10 · Evals + monitoring\"]\n  M10 --> M9\n  M2 --> M11[\"11 · Reasoning models\"]\n  M11 --> M5\n  M7 --> M12[\"12 · Faster inference\"]\n  M12 --> M9</pre>\n      <figcaption>Modules 0–2 are \"how models work\". Modules 3–5 are \"how to build with models\". Modules 6–9 are \"how to customise, ship and prove it\".</figcaption>\n    </figure>\n    <div class=\"gx-two\">\n      <div>\n        <h4>How to use each module</h4>\n        <ol>\n          <li>Read the notes. Say each key idea out loud in one sentence.</li>\n          <li>Type the code yourself. Do not copy-paste the first time.</li>\n          <li>Answer the practice questions before opening the answers.</li>\n          <li>Build the mini-project and push it to GitHub with a README.</li>\n          <li>Tick \"Module done\". It syncs to your account.</li>\n        </ol>\n      </div>\n      <div>\n        <h4>Setup once</h4>\n        <ul>\n          <li>Python 3.11+, <code>uv</code> or <code>venv</code>, VS Code or Jupyter.</li>\n          <li>Free GPU: Google Colab or Kaggle (T4). Paid: RunPod, Modal, Lambda.</li>\n          <li>One API key (Anthropic, OpenAI or Google). Put it in an env var, never in code.</li>\n          <li>Hugging Face account for open models and datasets.</li>\n          <li>Docker Desktop for Module 7 and the capstone.</li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"gx-code\"><div class=\"gx-code-head\"><span>setup.sh</span><button class=\"gx-copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">python -m venv .venv &amp;&amp; source .venv/bin/activate\npip install numpy torch scikit-learn matplotlib\npip install anthropic pydantic transformers datasets accelerate peft trl bitsandbytes\npip install sentence-transformers chromadb rank-bm25 langgraph langchain-anthropic \"mcp[cli]\"\npip install fastapi uvicorn langfuse faster-whisper diffusers\nexport ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=\"...\"   # keep keys in env vars or a .env file that git ignores</code></pre></div>\n  </article>\n\n  <article id=\"s-2\" class=\"card gx-slide\">\n    <div class=\"gx-slide-tag\"><b>Mindset</b> · what a strong Gen AI engineer does differently</div>\n    <h3>What matters vs what is hype</h3>\n    <div class=\"gx-table-wrap\"><table>\n      <thead><tr><th>Matters a lot</th><th>Why</th><th>Less than you think</th></tr></thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr><td>Evals</td><td>Without a test set you cannot tell if a change helped. Every senior interview asks about it.</td><td>Picking the \"best\" framework</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Retrieval quality</td><td>Most RAG failures are retrieval failures, not model failures.</td><td>Fancy prompt templates</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Context engineering</td><td>What you put in the window decides output quality more than wording tricks.</td><td>Magic phrases in prompts</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Cost and latency math</td><td>Production systems live or die on tokens per request and p95 latency.</td><td>Leaderboard rank of a model</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Security</td><td>Prompt injection and data leaks are real incidents, not theory.</td><td>Multi-agent swarms for simple tasks</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Knowing the internals</td><td>Attention, KV cache and tokenization explain most weird behaviour.</td><td>Fine-tuning before trying prompts and RAG</td></tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table></div>\n    <div class=\"gx-callout gx-key\"><span class=\"gx-label\">Rule of thumb</span><p>Climb the ladder in order and stop at the first rung that works: <strong>better prompt → better context (RAG) → tools/workflow → agent → fine-tune → train</strong>.</p></div>\n  </article>\n\n  <article id=\"s-3\" class=\"card gx-slide\">\n    <div class=\"gx-slide-tag\"><b>Outcomes</b> · what each module should leave you able to do</div>\n    <h3>You should now be able to...</h3>\n    <div class=\"gx-table-wrap\"><table>\n      <thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>After it, you can</th></tr></thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr><td>0 Neural nets</td><td>derive a gradient by hand, explain why ReLU, residuals and AdamW help, and spot over- vs underfitting</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>1 Architectures</td><td>pick CNN, RNN, transformer, SSM or diffusion for a problem and justify it by inductive bias</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>2 LLM internals</td><td>compute attention by hand, explain RoPE, the KV cache and FlashAttention, and count a model's parameters</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>3 APIs and context</td><td>budget a context window, use prompt caching, and get valid structured output with a retry loop</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>4 RAG and evals</td><td>build hybrid retrieval with RRF, measure recall@k and MRR, and name the failure point of a bad answer</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>5 Agents</td><td>write a tool loop, build it as a LangGraph graph with checkpoints and approvals, and explain MCP</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>6 Fine-tuning</td><td>decide prompt vs RAG vs fine-tune, estimate LoRA/QLoRA memory, and explain DPO and GRPO</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>7 Serving and security</td><td>explain prefill vs decode, continuous batching, the request path, and layered injection defences</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>8 Multimodal</td><td>estimate image token costs and describe vision, speech and diffusion pipelines</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>9 Capstone</td><td>ship DocuMind with evals and defend every design choice in an interview</td></tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table></div>\n  </article>"
